IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `pa-dates`

-- Probate and Administration Act 1898 (NSW) -- the provisions a person with a claim on a deceased person's estate meets,
-- from the seat of that person.
--
-- SEAT: the person with a claim on the estate of someone who has died in New South Wales: a beneficiary under the will
-- or on intestacy (including a legatee and a dependant), a family member who wants the estate administered, or a
-- creditor of the person who died. Every exported question is one that person would ask: "Can I be granted
-- administration?", "Can I lodge a caveat?", "The executor has done nothing: from when can I go to the Court?", "Can the
-- executor pay me maintenance now?", "From when can the executor distribute, and does that defeat my claim?", "The
-- executor disputes my claim: by when must I sue, and can my claim be barred?", "Can I apply to the Court for my
-- legacy, and at what rate does interest run?", "Can I still challenge the accounts?", "Can the commission be cut?",
-- "What happens if the executor files no accounts?", "In what order are the gifts used to pay debts?". The executor,
-- the administrator, the Court and the Registrar get no seat: what they may and must do is encoded as what it means for
-- the claimant.
--
-- Provisions: ss 32G, 63 (administration on intestacy); s 75 (the executor who does not prove the will); s 42(2)
-- (notice of an application); s 101 (small estates); s 144 (caveats); s 40B(3), (4) (grants on presumption of death);
-- s 81B (undisclosed property); s 92A (maintenance distributions); ss 92, 95 and Sch 5 cl 8 (distribution after
-- notice; following assets); s 93 (disputed claims barred); s 84 (application for a legacy); s 84A (interest on
-- legacies); s 85(3) (allowed accounts as a release); ss 86, 86A (commission); s 87 (accounts not filed); s 46C and
-- Sch 3 (solvency and the order of assets). Out, with reasons in NOTES.md: the rest of the Act.
--
-- ANSWERS. A yes/no question answers JUST TRUE, JUST FALSE where the Act's own words say no (a "means" definition, a
-- condition introduced by "if", "where" or "unless", a period the text fixes, a flat "shall not"), or NOTHING where
-- the Act does not answer on the facts given; each NOTHING carries a "NOTHING-kind" comment.
--
-- Based on content from the New South Wales Legislation website (as collected by the Open Australian Legal Corpus on
-- 10 March 2025; version as at 28 September 2020; unofficial, possibly out of date). CC BY 4.0. BASELINE ENCODING. Not
-- for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Probate and Administration Act 1898`

§§ `The nouns`

-- The person asking, by their relationship to the person who died (s 63). s 32G(2): "spouse" of an intestate includes
-- a de facto spouse at the time of death. "Next of kin" is not defined in the Act (a borrowed term, asked).
DECLARE `My relationship to the person who died` IS ONE OF
    `I was their spouse or de facto spouse`
    `I am one of their next of kin`
    `I am their creditor`
    `none of these`

-- Who is asking under s 75(1): "(i) any person interested in the estate, or (ii) the NSW Trustee or a trustee company,
-- or (iii) any creditor of the testator".
DECLARE `My standing under section 75` IS ONE OF
    `I am interested in the estate`
    `I am a creditor of the person who died`
    `neither`

-- Who the executor or administrator is (s 93(3) gives the NSW Trustee and trustee companies an extra step).
DECLARE `Who the executor or administrator is` IS ONE OF
    `the NSW Trustee and Guardian`
    `a trustee company`
    `someone else`

-- The categories of assets in Sch 3 Pt 2, in the Schedule's order.
DECLARE `The kind of asset` IS ONE OF
    `assets undisposed of by the will`
    `assets in a residuary gift and not specifically disposed of`
    `assets the will appropriates for paying debts`
    `assets the will charges with paying debts`
    `the fund kept back to meet pecuniary legacies`
    `assets specifically disposed of by the will`

-- The answer to "what does section 92 mean for my claim?"
DECLARE `What section 92 means for my claim` IS ONE OF
    `the executor is not protected by section 92 against my claim`
    `the executor is not liable to me for the assets distributed, but I may follow them into the hands of those who received them`
    `the earlier section 92 applies because the death was before the 2008 amendments, and it is not encoded here`

-- The answer to "what can happen to my claim the executor disputes?"
DECLARE `What section 93 means for my claim` IS ONE OF
    `section 93 does not apply to my claim`
    `the 3 months to take proceedings have not ended, so section 93 cannot yet bar my claim`
    `section 93 gives no bar while I satisfy the Court I am prosecuting my claim`
    `the Court may bar my claim against the executor on the executor's application`
    `the 2 months to serve process on the NSW Trustee or trustee company have not ended`
    `my claim is barred and irrecoverable against the NSW Trustee or trustee company`

-- The answer to "what happens if the executor has not filed the inventory or accounts?"
DECLARE `What section 87 means for the accounts` IS ONE OF
    `the time for filing has not passed by a month, so section 87 does not yet arise`
    `the executor filed within a month after the time fixed, so section 87 does not arise`
    `the Registrar must notify the executor of the neglect`
    `the executor has a month from the Registrar's notice to file`
    `the executor filed within a month of the Registrar's notice`
    `the Court may order the executor to show cause, on pain of contempt or a fine of up to 20 penalty units, with the costs personally`

-- The parties and acts of the regulative rules.
DECLARE `Estate party` IS ONE OF `the executor or administrator`, `the claimant`
DECLARE `Estate act` IS ONE OF
    `prove the will or renounce probate`
    `distribute the assets`
    `take proceedings to enforce the claim`
    `serve process on the NSW Trustee or trustee company`
    `file the inventory or accounts`

§§ `ss 32G, 63 -- administration on intestacy`

-- s 63 "The Court may grant administration of the estate of an intestate person to the following persons, not being
-- minors, that is to say to-- (a) the spouse of the deceased, or (b) one or more of the next of kin, or (c) the spouse
-- conjointly with one or more of the next of kin, or if there be no such person or no such person within the
-- jurisdiction-- (i) who is, of the opinion of the Court, fit to be so trusted, or (ii) who, upon being required ... to
-- apply for administration, complies ..., then to-- (d) any person, whether a creditor or not of the deceased, that the
-- Court thinks fit." s 3(1) "Minor means a person under the age of eighteen years." "Not being minors" is read over the
-- whole list, (d) included (fork F2). The (d) limb's condition ("if there be no such person") failing is JUST FALSE;
-- the Court's choice among those eligible is a judgment, so JUST TRUE means "the Court may grant it to me". A person
-- who left a will is outside s 63 (NOTHING).
@export Can the Court grant me administration of the estate of my relative who died without a will (ss 3, 32G, 63)?
GIVEN `my relative left a will` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who died left a will (s 63 deals only with someone who died without one)
      `my relationship` IS A `My relationship to the person who died` @desc Your relationship to the person who died. One of: "I was their spouse or de facto spouse" (married, or living in a de facto relationship with them when they died), "I am one of their next of kin", "I am their creditor", "none of these"
      `I am under 18` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are under 18 years of age
      `there is a fit spouse or next of kin in New South Wales` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether there is a spouse or next of kin of the person who died, aged 18 or over and in New South Wales, whom the Court thinks fit to be trusted with administration or who applied for it when required to (answer yes if you do not know)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I be granted administration` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `my relative left a will` THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (outside s 63's scope: an intestate person)
           IF `I am under 18` THEN JUST FALSE  -- "not being minors"
           IF `my relationship` EQUALS `I was their spouse or de facto spouse` THEN JUST TRUE  -- s 63(a), (c)
           IF `my relationship` EQUALS `I am one of their next of kin` THEN JUST TRUE  -- s 63(b), (c)
           IF `there is a fit spouse or next of kin in New South Wales` THEN JUST FALSE  -- "if there be no such person" fails
           OTHERWISE JUST TRUE  -- s 63(d): "any person, whether a creditor or not", that the Court thinks fit

§§ `s 75 -- the executor who does not prove the will`

-- s 75(1) "In any case where the executor named in a will-- (a) neglects or refuses to prove the same or to renounce
-- probate thereof within three months from the death of the testator or from the time of such executor attaining the
-- age of eighteen years, or (b) is unknown or cannot be found, the Court may upon the application of-- (i) any person
-- interested in the estate, or (ii) the NSW Trustee or a trustee company, or (iii) any creditor of the testator, order
-- that probate ... be granted to such executor or ... that administration with such will annexed be granted to the
-- applicant or make such other order for the administration of the estate as appears just." Written as a rule: the
-- executor must prove or renounce within the three months; time 0 is the day of death, or the executor's 18th birthday
-- where the executor was a minor at the death (fork F3).
GIVEN `the days in the three months` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Estate party` `Estate act`
`s 75(1)(a) -- the executor must prove the will or renounce probate within three months` `the days in the three months` MEANS
    PARTY `the executor or administrator`
    MUST `prove the will or renounce probate`
    WITHIN `the days in the three months`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the executor or administrator` BECAUSE "the executor named in the will neither proved it nor renounced probate within three months (s 75(1)(a))"

-- The day the three months start: the death, or the executor's 18th birthday if later.
GIVEN `the day of death` IS A DATE, `the executor was under 18 at the death` IS A BOOLEAN, `the executor's 18th birthday` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the day the s 75 three months start` `the day of death` `the executor was under 18 at the death` `the executor's 18th birthday` MEANS
    IF `the executor was under 18 at the death`
    THEN `the later of` `the day of death` `and` `the executor's 18th birthday`
    ELSE `the day of death`

@export If the executor named in the will has neither proved it nor renounced, from what day can I ask the Court to act (s 75(1)(a))?
GIVEN `the day my relative died` IS A DATE @desc The day the person who made the will died
      `the executor was under 18 at the death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the executor named in the will was under 18 when the person died
      `the executor's 18th birthday` IS A DATE @desc The executor's 18th birthday (any date if the executor was 18 or over at the death)
GIVETH A DATE
`From what day can I apply because the executor has not proved the will` MEANS
    ((`the day the s 75 three months start` `the day my relative died` `the executor was under 18 at the death` `the executor's 18th birthday`) `plus months` 3) `plus days` 1

@export Can I ask the Court to order a grant, or another order for the estate, because the executor named in the will has not proved it or cannot be found (s 75(1))?
GIVEN `my standing` IS A `My standing under section 75` @desc Your position. One of: "I am interested in the estate" (for example a beneficiary under the will), "I am a creditor of the person who died", "neither"
      `the executor is unknown or cannot be found` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the executor named in the will is unknown or cannot be found
      `the day my relative died` IS A DATE @desc The day the person who made the will died
      `the executor was under 18 at the death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the executor named in the will was under 18 when the person died
      `the executor's 18th birthday` IS A DATE @desc The executor's 18th birthday (any date if the executor was 18 or over at the death)
      `the executor has proved the will or renounced` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the executor has obtained probate of the will or renounced probate
      `the day the executor proved or renounced` IS A DATE @desc The day the executor obtained probate or renounced (any date if neither)
      `the day I would apply` IS A DATE @desc The day you would make the application to the Court
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I apply because the executor has not proved the will` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `my standing` EQUALS `neither` THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (a grant limited in its own wording to the applicants s 75(1) names; fork F4)
           IF `the executor is unknown or cannot be found` THEN JUST TRUE  -- s 75(1)(b)
           OTHERWISE
               CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`s 75(1)(a) -- the executor must prove the will or renounce probate within three months` `the three months`) 0 `the events`
               WHEN FULFILLED THEN JUST FALSE  -- proved or renounced in time: "in any case where" fails
               OTHERWISE
                   IF `the day I would apply` `is before` `the first day I can apply`
                   THEN JUST FALSE  -- the three months have not run
                   ELSE JUST TRUE
    WHERE
        `the start` MEANS `the day the s 75 three months start` `the day my relative died` `the executor was under 18 at the death` `the executor's 18th birthday`
        `the three months` MEANS `the days from` `the start` `to` (`the start` `plus months` 3)
        `the first day I can apply` MEANS (`the start` `plus months` 3) `plus days` 1
        `the events` MEANS
            IF `the executor has proved the will or renounced`
            THEN LIST (EVENT `the executor or administrator` `prove the will or renounce probate` (`the days from` `the start` `to` `the day the executor proved or renounced`))
            ELSE LIST (WAITUNTIL (`the days from` `the start` `to` `the day I would apply`))

§§ `ss 42(2), 101, 144 -- applying for a grant, small estates, caveats`

-- s 42(2) "Notice of an application is to be published in the manner prescribed by the rules-- ... (b) in any other
-- case [than a cross-claim]--not less than 14 days before the making of the application."
@export If I apply for probate or letters of administration, what is the first day I can make the application after publishing the notice (s 42(2)(b))?
GIVEN `the day I published the notice` IS A DATE @desc The day the notice of your intended application was published as the court rules require (an application that is not a cross-claim)
GIVETH A DATE
`First day I can apply after publishing notice` MEANS `the day I published the notice` `plus days` 14

-- s 101 "In all cases where a person dies leaving property not exceeding $15,000 in value or, where some other amount
-- is prescribed, not exceeding that other amount so prescribed in value, application for probate or administration may
-- be made to the Registrar or to a district agent for the Registrar." A "where": over the amount is JUST FALSE. No
-- regulation is deposited; the Act's $15,000 is used (a prescribed amount would change the answer; NOTES.md).
`the section 101 amount` MEANS 15000

@export Can I apply for probate or administration of a small estate to the Registrar or a district agent, rather than to the Court (s 101)?
GIVEN `the value of the property left` IS A NUMBER @desc The value of the property the person left, in Australian dollars
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I apply to the Registrar for a small estate` MEANS
    IF `the value of the property left` AT MOST `the section 101 amount` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 144(1) "Any person may lodge in the registry of the Court a caveat against any application for probate or
-- administration ... at any time previous to such probate or administration being granted". A period fixed in the
-- text's own words: after the grant, JUST FALSE.
@export Can I lodge a caveat against an application for probate or administration of the estate (s 144(1))?
GIVEN `probate or administration has already been granted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether probate or letters of administration have already been granted on the application
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I lodge a caveat` MEANS
    IF `probate or administration has already been granted` THEN JUST FALSE ELSE JUST TRUE

§§ `ss 40B, 81B -- when the estate cannot be distributed or transferred`

-- s 40B(3) "The estate shall not be distributed without the leave of the Court." s 40B(4) "If a caveat is duly lodged
-- within such time as may be specified, the executor or administrator shall not distribute the estate until the caveat
-- is withdrawn or removed." Flat negatives: JUST FALSE. With leave and no caveat outstanding, JUST TRUE (on the
-- conditions the leave sets).
@export Where the grant was made on presumption of death only, can the estate be distributed now (s 40B(3), (4))?
GIVEN `the Court has given leave to distribute` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court has given leave to distribute the estate (in the grant or by a later order)
      `a caveat against distribution is outstanding` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a caveat against distribution was lodged within the time the Court specified and has not been withdrawn or removed
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the estate presumed dead be distributed` MEANS
    IF `the Court has given leave to distribute` AND (NOT `a caveat against distribution is outstanding`)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 81B(1) "... such an executor, administrator or trustee shall not complete the disposition of, any property of the
-- deceased vested in the deceased which has not been disclosed to the Court pursuant to section 81A (1) or (2)" (deaths
-- on or after 31 December 1981). A flat negative: JUST FALSE. Disclosed property, or an earlier death: s 81B is
-- silent (NOTHING).
@export Can the executor complete the transfer to me of property of the estate that has not been disclosed to the Court (s 81B(1))?
GIVEN `my relative died on or after 31 December 1981` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person died on or after 31 December 1981
      `the property has been disclosed to the Court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the property has been disclosed to the Court with the application for the grant or afterwards (s 81A)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the executor complete the transfer to me` MEANS
    IF `my relative died on or after 31 December 1981` AND (NOT `the property has been disclosed to the Court`)
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (the text is silent: s 81B bars only undisclosed property)

§§ `s 92A -- maintenance before the estate is distributed (if you depended on the person who died)`

-- s 92A(1) "This section applies if a person (the survivor)-- (a) survives a deceased person, and (b) at the time of
-- the deceased person's death, was wholly or substantially dependent on the deceased person, and (c) will be entitled to
-- part or all of the deceased person's estate if the person survives the deceased person for 30 days or ... the period
-- appearing in the will". (2) the executor "may make a distribution that is an adequate amount for the proper
-- maintenance, support or education of the survivor at any time after the death ..., including within 30 days". (3) even
-- knowing of a pending family provision application. An "if": a failed condition is JUST FALSE.
@export I depended on the person who died: can the executor pay me an amount for my maintenance, support or education now, even within the first 30 days (s 92A)?
GIVEN `I survived the person who died` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you survived the person who died
      `I was wholly or substantially dependent on them` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, when they died, you were wholly or substantially dependent on the person who died
      `I will be entitled if I survive them by the survival period` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you will be entitled to part or all of the estate if you survive the person by 30 days (or the period the will sets)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the executor pay me maintenance now` MEANS
    IF `I survived the person who died` AND `I was wholly or substantially dependent on them` AND `I will be entitled if I survive them by the survival period`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 92A(5) "An amount distributed under subsection (2) to a survivor must be deducted from any share of the estate to
-- which the survivor becomes entitled." The Act is silent where the maintenance exceeds the share (NOTHING).
@export If the executor paid me maintenance under section 92A, how much of my share is left to me (s 92A(5))?
GIVEN `my share of the estate` IS A NUMBER @desc The value of the share of the estate you become entitled to, in Australian dollars
      `the maintenance paid to me` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount the executor paid you for maintenance, support or education under section 92A, in Australian dollars
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`How much of my share is left after maintenance` MEANS
    IF `the maintenance paid to me` GREATER THAN `my share of the estate`
    THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (the text is silent where the maintenance exceeds the share)
    ELSE JUST (`my share of the estate` MINUS `the maintenance paid to me`)

§§ `ss 92, 95 -- distribution after notice, and following the assets`

-- s 92(1) the executor or administrator "may distribute the assets ... among the persons entitled having regard to the
-- claims ... of which the executor or administrator has notice at the time of distribution if-- (a) the assets are
-- distributed at least 6 months after the testator's or intestate's death, and (b) the executor or administrator has
-- given notice in the form approved ... that [they intend] to distribute the assets ... after the expiration of a
-- specified time, and (c) the time specified in the notice is not less than 30 days after the notice is given, and (d) the
-- time specified in the notice has expired." The specified time is taken as ending on a day the notice names; it has
-- expired from the next day (fork F5). Written as a bar: until the later of the two days, the executor shall not
-- distribute free of claims; time 0 is the day of death.
GIVEN `the days before the first protected day` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Estate party` `Estate act`
`s 92(1) -- the executor shall not distribute free of unnotified claims before six months and the notice's time have passed` `the days before the first protected day` MEANS
    PARTY `the executor or administrator`
    SHANT `distribute the assets`
    WITHIN `the days before the first protected day`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the executor or administrator` BECAUSE "the assets were distributed less than six months after the death or before the time in the notice expired (s 92(1)(a), (d))"

-- The first day a distribution meets s 92(1)(a) and (d); NOTHING where the notice's time is under 30 days, (c) failing.
GIVEN `the day of death` IS A DATE, `the day the notice was given` IS A DATE, `the last day of the time in the notice` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`the first day of a section 92 distribution` `the day of death` `the day the notice was given` `the last day of the time in the notice` MEANS
    IF `the days from` `the day the notice was given` `to` `the last day of the time in the notice` LESS THAN 30
    THEN NOTHING  -- s 92(1)(c) fails: the notice cannot support a s 92 distribution at all
    ELSE JUST (`the later of` (`the day of death` `plus months` 6) `and` (`the last day of the time in the notice` `plus days` 1))

@export If the executor has given notice of intended distribution, from what day can they distribute the estate under section 92 (s 92(1))? No answer means the notice's time is under 30 days, so it cannot support a section 92 distribution.
GIVEN `the day my relative died` IS A DATE @desc The day the person died
      `the day the notice was given` IS A DATE @desc The day the executor or administrator gave (published) the notice of intended distribution
      `the last day of the time in the notice` IS A DATE @desc The last day of the time the notice specifies, after which the executor intends to distribute
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`From what day can the executor distribute under section 92` MEANS
    `the first day of a section 92 distribution` `the day my relative died` `the day the notice was given` `the last day of the time in the notice`

-- s 92(2) the executor who distributes in accordance with (1) "is not liable in respect of those assets ... to any
-- person who has a claim in respect of those assets ... unless the executor or administrator had notice of the claim at
-- the time of the distribution or the distribution was not made in the circumstances described in [Succession Act 2006
-- ss 28(2)(a) or (b), 125, 126 or Part 4.4]". The second "unless" is read as reaching only claims under those
-- Succession Act provisions (fork F6; finding 2). s 92(3): deemed notice of a claim a Births, Deaths and Marriages
-- certificate under s 50 would have shown (deaths after the Children (Equality of Status) Act 1976 commenced). s 40B(4):
-- no benefit of s 92 where the Court directed notices on a grant on presumption of death and they were not given. Sch 5
-- cl 8: for a death before the Succession Amendment (Family Provision) Act 2008 commenced, the earlier s 92(1) applies.
-- s 95 "Nothing contained in section 92, 93 or 94 prejudices the right of any beneficiary, creditor or other person who
-- has a claim ... to follow those assets ... into the hands of the persons ... among whom those assets ... may have been
-- distributed".
@export If the executor distributes the estate after giving notice, can they still be liable to me for my claim, and can I follow the assets (ss 40B(4), 92, 95, Sch 5 cl 8)?
GIVEN `the day my relative died` IS A DATE @desc The day the person died
      `my relative died before the 2008 family provision amendments commenced` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person died before Schedule 2.9 of the Succession Amendment (Family Provision) Act 2008 commenced (the earlier rule then applies)
      `the executor gave notice of intended distribution` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the executor or administrator gave notice, in the approved form, that they intend to distribute after a specified time
      `the day the notice was given` IS A DATE @desc The day the notice was given (any date if none)
      `the last day of the time in the notice` IS A DATE @desc The last day of the time the notice specifies (any date if none)
      `the day of distribution` IS A DATE @desc The day the executor distributed the assets
      `the executor knew of my claim when distributing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the executor had notice of your claim when they distributed
      `a birth, death and marriage certificate would have shown my entitlement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your entitlement would have become apparent from a certificate under section 50 of the Births, Deaths and Marriages Registration Act 1995, and the person died after the Children (Equality of Status) Act 1976 commenced
      `my claim is under Succession Act section 28, 125, 126 or Part 4.4` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim arises from rectification of the will (s 28), sharing between spouses (s 125), a distribution order (s 126) or an Indigenous person's estate (Part 4.4) under the Succession Act 2006
      `the distribution met that Succession Act provision's conditions` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If your claim is under one of those Succession Act provisions, whether the distribution was made in the circumstances that provision describes
      `the Court directed notices that were not given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the grant was made on presumption of death and the Court directed notices before distribution that the executor did not give
GIVETH A `What section 92 means for my claim`
`What section 92 means for my claim` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `my relative died before the 2008 family provision amendments commenced` THEN `the earlier section 92 applies because the death was before the 2008 amendments, and it is not encoded here`
           IF (NOT `the executor gave notice of intended distribution`) THEN `the executor is not protected by section 92 against my claim`
           IF `the Court directed notices that were not given` THEN `the executor is not protected by section 92 against my claim`
           IF `the executor knew of my claim when distributing` THEN `the executor is not protected by section 92 against my claim`
           IF `a birth, death and marriage certificate would have shown my entitlement` THEN `the executor is not protected by section 92 against my claim`
           IF `my claim is under Succession Act section 28, 125, 126 or Part 4.4` AND (NOT `the distribution met that Succession Act provision's conditions`) THEN `the executor is not protected by section 92 against my claim`
           OTHERWISE
               CONSIDER `the first day of a section 92 distribution` `the day my relative died` `the day the notice was given` `the last day of the time in the notice`
               WHEN JUST `the first day` THEN
                   CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`s 92(1) -- the executor shall not distribute free of unnotified claims before six months and the notice's time have passed` ((`the days from` `the day my relative died` `to` `the first day`) MINUS 1)) 0 (LIST (EVENT `the executor or administrator` `distribute the assets` (`the days from` `the day my relative died` `to` `the day of distribution`)))
                   WHEN FULFILLED THEN `the executor is not liable to me for the assets distributed, but I may follow them into the hands of those who received them`
                   OTHERWISE `the executor is not protected by section 92 against my claim`
               OTHERWISE `the executor is not protected by section 92 against my claim`

§§ `s 93 -- a claim the executor disputes (if the executor has disputed your claim)`

-- s 93(1) once the s 92(1) notices are published and a claim is submitted that the executor disputes, the executor
-- "may ... serve on the person ... a notice calling on the person to take proceedings to enforce the person's claim
-- within a period of 3 months from the date of service of the notice and to prosecute the person's claim." (2) if, after
-- the 3 months, the person "does not satisfy the Court that the person is prosecuting the person's claim, the Court
-- may, on an application ... by the executor or administrator-- (a) make an order barring the claim ... or (b) make such
-- other order ...". (3) where the NSW Trustee or a trustee company has served the s 93(1) notice and the claimant "has
-- not, within the period of 3 months ..., commenced proceedings", it may serve a further notice that unless within 2
-- months it "is duly served with process", it will distribute without regard to the claim. (4) if not served within the
-- 2 months, "the claimant's claim shall thereupon be barred and become irrecoverable" against it. (5) it may waive the
-- objection. Time 0 of each rule is the day of service of its notice.
GIVEN `the days in the three months` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Estate party` `Estate act`
`s 93(1) -- the claimant is called on to take proceedings within three months` `the days in the three months` MEANS
    PARTY `the claimant`
    MUST `take proceedings to enforce the claim`
    WITHIN `the days in the three months`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the claimant` BECAUSE "proceedings to enforce the disputed claim were not taken within 3 months of service of the notice (s 93(1))"

GIVEN `the days in the two months` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Estate party` `Estate act`
`s 93(3), (4) -- the claimant must serve process on the NSW Trustee or trustee company within two months` `the days in the two months` MEANS
    PARTY `the claimant`
    MUST `serve process on the NSW Trustee or trustee company`
    WITHIN `the days in the two months`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the claimant` BECAUSE "process in proceedings to enforce the claim was not served on the NSW Trustee or trustee company within 2 months of the further notice (s 93(4))"

@export If the executor disputes my claim and serves a notice under section 93, by what day must I take proceedings to enforce it (s 93(1))?
GIVEN `the day the notice was served on me` IS A DATE @desc The day the executor's notice calling on you to take proceedings was served on you
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I take proceedings on my disputed claim` MEANS `the day the notice was served on me` `plus months` 3

@export The executor disputes my claim on the estate: on the facts so far, can my claim be barred under section 93 (s 93)?
GIVEN `the executor published the section 92 notices` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the executor or administrator published the notice of intended distribution under section 92
      `the executor disputes my claim and served a section 93 notice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the executor disputes your claim and served on you a notice calling on you to take proceedings within 3 months
      `the day the notice was served on me` IS A DATE @desc The day that notice was served on you (any date if none)
      `I have commenced proceedings` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have commenced court proceedings to enforce your claim
      `the day I commenced proceedings` IS A DATE @desc The day you commenced them (any date if not)
      `the Court is satisfied I am prosecuting my claim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you can satisfy the Court that you are prosecuting your claim (actively pursuing the proceedings)
      `who the executor is` IS A `Who the executor or administrator is` @desc Who the executor or administrator is. One of: "the NSW Trustee and Guardian", "a trustee company", "someone else"
      `a further notice was served on me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the NSW Trustee or trustee company served on you a further notice that it will distribute unless served with process within 2 months
      `the day the further notice was served on me` IS A DATE @desc The day the further notice was served on you (any date if none)
      `I served process on it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have served court process in your proceedings on the NSW Trustee or trustee company
      `the day I served process` IS A DATE @desc The day you served it (any date if not)
      `it waived the objection` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the NSW Trustee or trustee company has waived its objection to proceedings commenced after the 2 months
      `the day I am asking about` IS A DATE @desc The day you are asking about (for example today)
GIVETH A `What section 93 means for my claim`
`Can my disputed claim be barred` MEANS
    BRANCH IF (NOT `the executor published the section 92 notices`) THEN `section 93 does not apply to my claim`
           IF (NOT `the executor disputes my claim and served a section 93 notice`) THEN `section 93 does not apply to my claim`
           IF `the day I am asking about` `is before` `the first day after the three months` THEN `the 3 months to take proceedings have not ended, so section 93 cannot yet bar my claim`
           IF `the trustee route applies` THEN
               CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`s 93(3), (4) -- the claimant must serve process on the NSW Trustee or trustee company within two months` `the two months`) 0 `the process events`
               WHEN FULFILLED THEN `the s 93(2) answer`
               OTHERWISE
                   IF `the day I am asking about` `is before` `the first day after the two months`
                   THEN `the 2 months to serve process on the NSW Trustee or trustee company have not ended`
                   ELSE IF `it waived the objection`
                        THEN `the s 93(2) answer`
                        ELSE `my claim is barred and irrecoverable against the NSW Trustee or trustee company`
           OTHERWISE `the s 93(2) answer`
    WHERE
        `the end of the three months` MEANS `the day the notice was served on me` `plus months` 3
        `the first day after the three months` MEANS `the end of the three months` `plus days` 1
        `the three months` MEANS `the days from` `the day the notice was served on me` `to` `the end of the three months`
        `the proceedings events` MEANS
            IF `I have commenced proceedings`
            THEN LIST (EVENT `the claimant` `take proceedings to enforce the claim` (`the days from` `the day the notice was served on me` `to` `the day I commenced proceedings`))
            ELSE LIST (WAITUNTIL (`the days from` `the day the notice was served on me` `to` `the day I am asking about`))
        `I commenced in the three months` MEANS
            CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`s 93(1) -- the claimant is called on to take proceedings within three months` `the three months`) 0 `the proceedings events`
            WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
            OTHERWISE FALSE
        `the trustee route applies` MEANS
                (NOT (`who the executor is` EQUALS `someone else`))
            AND (NOT `I commenced in the three months`)
            AND `a further notice was served on me`
        `the end of the two months` MEANS `the day the further notice was served on me` `plus months` 2
        `the first day after the two months` MEANS `the end of the two months` `plus days` 1
        `the two months` MEANS `the days from` `the day the further notice was served on me` `to` `the end of the two months`
        `the process events` MEANS
            IF `I served process on it`
            THEN LIST (EVENT `the claimant` `serve process on the NSW Trustee or trustee company` (`the days from` `the day the further notice was served on me` `to` `the day I served process`))
            ELSE LIST (WAITUNTIL (`the days from` `the day the further notice was served on me` `to` `the day I am asking about`))
        `the s 93(2) answer` MEANS
            IF `the Court is satisfied I am prosecuting my claim`
            THEN `section 93 gives no bar while I satisfy the Court I am prosecuting my claim`
            ELSE `the Court may bar my claim against the executor on the executor's application`

§§ `ss 84, 84A -- legacies (if you are left a legacy or a devise)`

-- s 84 "If the executor or administrator, after requesting in writing, neglects or refuses to-- (a) sign such
-- acknowledgment, or (b) execute a conveyance of land devised to the devisee, or (c) pay or hand over to the person
-- entitled any legacy or residuary bequest, the Court may, on the application of such devisee or person, make such order
-- in the matter as it may think fit." An "if": without a written request, or before a neglect or refusal, JUST FALSE.
@export Can I apply to the Court because the executor has not paid or handed over my legacy, or conveyed or acknowledged my devise, after I asked in writing (s 84)?
GIVEN `I asked the executor in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you asked the executor or administrator in writing to pay or hand over the legacy or residuary bequest, or to convey or sign an acknowledgment for the land left to you
      `the executor neglected or refused` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, after your written request, the executor or administrator neglected or refused to do so
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I apply to the Court for my legacy` MEANS
    IF `I asked the executor in writing` AND `the executor neglected or refused` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 84A(1) where interest is payable on a legacy or arrears of an annuity under the will or instrument "or with any
-- enactment or rule of law, that interest shall, unless the will or instrument otherwise provides, or the Court otherwise
-- orders, be payable at the relevant rate or such other rate as may be prescribed in a regulation". (3) "relevant rate
-- of interest means the rate that lies 2% above the cash rate last published by the Reserve Bank of Australia before 1
-- January in the calendar year in which interest begins to accrue." Whether interest is payable at all is the will or the
-- general law (asked). No regulation is deposited; a prescribed rate would change the answer (NOTES.md). Where the will
-- and a Court order both set a rate, the order is taken (fork F7).
`the margin over the cash rate` MEANS 2

@export At what yearly rate (in per cent) is interest payable on my legacy or on arrears of my annuity (s 84A(1), (3))?
GIVEN `interest is payable on my legacy` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether interest is payable on your legacy or annuity arrears at all, under the will, another instrument, an Act or the general law
      `the will sets the rate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will or instrument itself sets the rate of interest
      `the rate the will sets` IS A NUMBER @desc The yearly rate the will or instrument sets, in per cent (any number if none)
      `the Court has ordered a rate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court has ordered a different rate
      `the rate the Court ordered` IS A NUMBER @desc The yearly rate the Court ordered, in per cent (any number if none)
      `the cash rate before 1 January` IS A NUMBER @desc The Reserve Bank of Australia cash rate last published before 1 January of the year in which the interest begins to accrue, in per cent (for example 4.35)
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`What rate of interest is payable on my legacy` MEANS
    BRANCH IF (NOT `interest is payable on my legacy`) THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (s 84A sets a rate only where interest is payable; whether it is points outside the Act)
           IF `the Court has ordered a rate` THEN JUST `the rate the Court ordered`
           IF `the will sets the rate` THEN JUST `the rate the will sets`
           OTHERWISE JUST (`the cash rate before 1 January` PLUS `the margin over the cash rate`)

-- s 84A(2) where the executor "appropriates any property in or towards satisfaction of any legacy (other than an
-- annuity), the legatee shall be entitled to the income from the property so appropriated, and interest shall not be
-- payable out of any other part of the estate on so much of the legacy as has been satisfied by the appropriation."
@export On how much of my legacy can interest still run, if the executor has appropriated property towards it (s 84A(2))?
GIVEN `my legacy is an annuity` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your legacy is an annuity (s 84A(2) does not apply to annuities)
      `the amount of my legacy` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount of your legacy, in Australian dollars
      `the amount satisfied by appropriated property` IS A NUMBER @desc How much of your legacy the executor has satisfied by appropriating property to you, in Australian dollars (0 if none)
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`On how much of my legacy can interest run` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `my legacy is an annuity` THEN JUST `the amount of my legacy`
           IF `the amount satisfied by appropriated property` AT LEAST `the amount of my legacy` THEN JUST 0
           OTHERWISE JUST (`the amount of my legacy` MINUS `the amount satisfied by appropriated property`)

§§ `ss 85(3), 86, 86A, 87 -- accounts and commission`

-- s 85(3) "The order of the Court allowing any such account shall be prima facie evidence of the correctness of the
-- same, and shall, after the expiration of three years from the date of such order, operate as a release to the person
-- filing the same, excepting so far as it is shown by some person interested therein that an error or omission or
-- fraudulent entry has been made in such account." The three years end on the same date three years on; the release
-- operates from the next day (forks F1, F8).
@export From what day does the Court's order allowing the executor's accounts operate as a release to the executor (s 85(3))?
GIVEN `the day of the order allowing the accounts` IS A DATE @desc The day the Court made the order allowing the executor's, administrator's or trustee's accounts
GIVETH A DATE
`From what day do allowed accounts operate as a release` MEANS (`the day of the order allowing the accounts` `plus months` 36) `plus days` 1

@export Can I still challenge the executor's accounts that the Court has allowed (s 85(3))?
GIVEN `the day of the order allowing the accounts` IS A DATE @desc The day the Court made the order allowing the accounts
      `the day I would challenge them` IS A DATE @desc The day you would challenge the accounts
      `I can show an error, omission or fraudulent entry` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you, as a person interested in the estate, can show that an error, omission or fraudulent entry was made in the accounts
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I still challenge the allowed accounts` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `I can show an error, omission or fraudulent entry` THEN JUST TRUE  -- "excepting so far as it is shown"
           IF `the day I would challenge them` `is before` (`From what day do allowed accounts operate as a release` `the day of the order allowing the accounts`) THEN JUST TRUE  -- only prima facie evidence before the release
           OTHERWISE JUST FALSE  -- the order operates as a release

-- s 86(1) the Court "may allow ... such commission or percentage ... as is just and reasonable". (2) "No such allowance
-- shall be made to any executor, administrator, or trustee who neglects or omits without good reason to pass the
-- accounts". A flat "no ... shall": JUST FALSE. Otherwise the Court's judgment, asked: allowed is JUST TRUE; not
-- allowed is NOTHING (a grant limited in its own words).
@export Can the executor be paid commission out of the estate for their pains and trouble (s 86(1), (2))?
GIVEN `the executor neglected to pass the accounts without good reason` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the executor, administrator or trustee neglected or omitted, without good reason, to pass the accounts as the rules or a Court order require
      `the Court allows a commission as just and reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court, in passing the accounts, allows a commission it finds just and reasonable
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the executor be paid commission` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `the executor neglected to pass the accounts without good reason` THEN JUST FALSE
           IF `the Court allows a commission as just and reasonable` THEN JUST TRUE
           OTHERWISE NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (a grant limited in its own wording, "just and reasonable"; the case falls outside it)

-- s 86A "Where the Court is of the opinion that a commission or amount charged or proposed to be charged ... is
-- excessive, the Court may, of its own motion, or on the motion of any person interested in the estate, review the
-- commission ... and may ... notwithstanding any provision contained in a will authorising the charging of the
-- commission ... reduce" it. A "where": if the Court does not find it excessive, JUST FALSE.
@export Can the commission the executor charges the estate be reduced, even if the will authorises it (s 86A)? Anyone interested in the estate may ask the Court to review it.
GIVEN `the Court finds the commission excessive` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court is of the opinion that the commission or amount charged, or proposed to be charged, is excessive
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the commission be reduced` MEANS
    IF `the Court finds the commission excessive` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 87(1) where the executor neglects to file the inventory or accounts as s 85 requires "within one month after the
-- expiration of the time fixed for compliance, the Registrar shall cause the executor ... to be notified". (2) where,
-- "on the expiration of one month after having been so notified", the executor "further neglects", the Court may order
-- them to show cause. (3) failing to show cause: contempt, or "a fine not exceeding 20 penalty units". (4) personally
-- liable for the costs unless the Court otherwise orders. Two rules; time 0 is the last day fixed for compliance, and
-- then the day of the Registrar's notice.
GIVEN `the days in the month` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Estate party` `Estate act`
`s 87(1) -- the executor must file within a month after the time fixed` `the days in the month` MEANS
    PARTY `the executor or administrator`
    MUST `file the inventory or accounts`
    WITHIN `the days in the month`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the executor or administrator` BECAUSE "the inventory or accounts were not filed within one month after the time fixed (s 87(1))"

GIVEN `the days in the month` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Estate party` `Estate act`
`s 87(2) -- the executor must file within a month after the Registrar's notice` `the days in the month` MEANS
    PARTY `the executor or administrator`
    MUST `file the inventory or accounts`
    WITHIN `the days in the month`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the executor or administrator` BECAUSE "the inventory or accounts were still not filed one month after the Registrar's notice (s 87(2))"

`the most penalty units under s 87(3)` MEANS 20

@export The executor has not filed the inventory or accounts on time: what can happen now (s 87)?
GIVEN `the last day fixed for filing` IS A DATE @desc The last day of the time the court rules or an order fixed for filing the inventory or accounts
      `the executor has filed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the executor, administrator or trustee has now filed (or verified and filed, or passed) what was required
      `the day the executor filed` IS A DATE @desc The day they filed it (any date if not)
      `the Registrar has notified the executor` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Registrar has notified the executor of the neglect
      `the day of the Registrar's notice` IS A DATE @desc The day of that notice (any date if none)
      `the day I am asking about` IS A DATE @desc The day you are asking about (for example today)
GIVETH A `What section 87 means for the accounts`
`What can happen if the executor files no accounts` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `filed in the first month` THEN `the executor filed within a month after the time fixed, so section 87 does not arise`
           IF `the day I am asking about` `is before` (`the end of the first month` `plus days` 1) THEN `the time for filing has not passed by a month, so section 87 does not yet arise`
           IF (NOT `the Registrar has notified the executor`) THEN `the Registrar must notify the executor of the neglect`
           OTHERWISE
               CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`s 87(2) -- the executor must file within a month after the Registrar's notice` (`the days from` `the day of the Registrar's notice` `to` `the end of the second month`)) 0 `the second events`
               WHEN FULFILLED THEN `the executor filed within a month of the Registrar's notice`
               OTHERWISE
                   IF `the day I am asking about` `is before` (`the end of the second month` `plus days` 1)
                   THEN `the executor has a month from the Registrar's notice to file`
                   ELSE `the Court may order the executor to show cause, on pain of contempt or a fine of up to 20 penalty units, with the costs personally`
    WHERE
        `the end of the first month` MEANS `the last day fixed for filing` `plus months` 1
        `the end of the second month` MEANS `the day of the Registrar's notice` `plus months` 1
        `the first events` MEANS
            IF `the executor has filed`
            THEN LIST (EVENT `the executor or administrator` `file the inventory or accounts` (`the days from` `the last day fixed for filing` `to` `the day the executor filed`))
            ELSE LIST (WAITUNTIL (`the days from` `the last day fixed for filing` `to` `the day I am asking about`))
        `filed in the first month` MEANS
            CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`s 87(1) -- the executor must file within a month after the time fixed` (`the days from` `the last day fixed for filing` `to` `the end of the first month`)) 0 `the first events`
            WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
            OTHERWISE FALSE
        `the second events` MEANS
            IF `the executor has filed`
            THEN LIST (EVENT `the executor or administrator` `file the inventory or accounts` (`the days from` `the day of the Registrar's notice` `to` `the day the executor filed`))
            ELSE LIST (WAITUNTIL (`the days from` `the day of the Registrar's notice` `to` `the day I am asking about`))

@export If the executor fails to show cause under section 87, what is the most they can be fined, in penalty units (s 87(3))?
GIVETH A NUMBER
`The most the executor can be fined in penalty units` MEANS `the most penalty units under s 87(3)`

§§ `s 46C, Sch 3 -- paying the debts out of the estate`

-- s 46C(3) "solvent means sufficient and insolvent means insufficient for the payment in full of the debts and
-- liabilities of the deceased." A "means" definition.
@export Is the estate solvent, that is, enough to pay the debts and liabilities of the person who died in full (s 46C(3))?
GIVEN `the value of the estate` IS A NUMBER @desc The value of the real and personal estate of the person who died, in Australian dollars
      `the debts and liabilities` IS A NUMBER @desc The total debts and liabilities of the person who died, in Australian dollars
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is the estate solvent` MEANS
    IF `the value of the estate` AT LEAST `the debts and liabilities` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 46C(2) where the estate is solvent, it is applicable towards the funeral, testamentary and administrative expenses,
-- debts and liabilities "subject to ... the provisions, if any, contained in the deceased person's will ... in the order
-- mentioned in Part 2 of the Third Schedule". Sch 3 Pt 2 lists six classes (1 first). Insolvent estates go by Sch 3 Pt 1
-- (funeral, testamentary and administration expenses first, then the bankruptcy rules): not an order of gifts, NOTHING.
-- A will that sets its own order: NOTHING (the will, not the Act, decides).
@export If the estate is solvent, in what place (1 = used first, 6 = used last) is the kind of asset left to me used to pay the funeral and other expenses, debts and liabilities (s 46C(2), Sch 3 Pt 2)?
GIVEN `the kind of asset` IS A `The kind of asset` @desc The kind of asset you are asking about. One of: "assets undisposed of by the will", "assets in a residuary gift and not specifically disposed of", "assets the will appropriates for paying debts", "assets the will charges with paying debts", "the fund kept back to meet pecuniary legacies", "assets specifically disposed of by the will"
      `the estate is solvent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the estate is enough to pay all the debts and liabilities in full
      `the will sets its own order for paying debts` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will itself says which assets are to pay the debts and in what order
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`In what place is my gift used to pay the debts` MEANS
    BRANCH IF (NOT `the estate is solvent`) THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (Sch 3 Pt 2 is only for solvent estates; Pt 1 gives no order of gifts)
           IF `the will sets its own order for paying debts` THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the Act, to the will)
           OTHERWISE
               CONSIDER `the kind of asset`
               WHEN `assets undisposed of by the will` THEN JUST 1
               WHEN `assets in a residuary gift and not specifically disposed of` THEN JUST 2
               WHEN `assets the will appropriates for paying debts` THEN JUST 3
               WHEN `assets the will charges with paying debts` THEN JUST 4
               WHEN `the fund kept back to meet pecuniary legacies` THEN JUST 5
               WHEN `assets specifically disposed of by the will` THEN JUST 6
